# Shape reference for web/.env — dummy values only, never the real thing.
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
# Vite only exposes env vars prefixed VITE_ to client code, and everything
# here ends up in the built JS bundle — none of these are secret in the way
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is. Sentry's DSN is write-only (it can send events, not
# read them) and PostHog's project API key is designed to be public. Neither
# needs the protection backend/.env.example's keys do.
#
# In production these are baked in at build time (see .github/workflows), not
# read at runtime — there is no .env file on GitHub Pages / Cloudflare Pages.

# Error tracking. Leave unset locally if you don't want dev-machine noise in
# Sentry. Create at https://sentry.io — reuse the org from the backend setup,
# add a new project with platform "Browser JavaScript" (a separate project
# from the backend one, since they're different runtimes). Get the DSN from
# that project's Settings → Client Keys (DSN).
#VITE_SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@<org>.ingest.sentry.io/<project>

# Analytics. Create a free project at https://posthog.com. Get the key from
# Project Settings → Project API Key (starts with phc_). The host depends on
# which region you signed up in — check Project Settings if unsure.
#VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_REPLACE_ME
#VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
